---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Campaign page for the November 2026 Roselle Park mayoral and council races.
// Figures checked 2026-09-26; each claim links to its source.
const src = {
  pjm2526: 'https://www.pjm.com/-/media/DotCom/markets-ops/rpm/rpm-auction-info/2025-2026/2025-2026-base-residual-auction-report.pdf',
  pjm2728: 'https://insidelines.pjm.com/pjm-auction-procures-134479-mw-of-generation-resources/',
  pjm2829: 'https://insidelines.pjm.com/pjm-capacity-auction-procures-138318-mw-of-generation-resources-as-work-continues-to-address-growing-electricity-demand/',
  bgs2025: 'https://www.nj.gov/bpu/newsroom/2025/approved/20250212.html',
  bgs2026: 'https://nj.gov/bpu/newsroom/2026/approved/20260212.html',
  county: 'https://ucnj.org/press-releases/public-info/2012/09/21/union-county-solar-program-completes-28-installations-in-15-governmental-agencies/',
  esip: 'https://www.sustainablejersey.com/fileadmin/media/Grants_and_Resources/ESIP_HowToGuide_V2_2019.pdf',
  adi: 'https://www.nj.gov/bpu/pdf/boardorders/2026/20260521/8C%20ORDER%20ADI%203%20Year%20Review%20Cost%20Cap.pdf',
  obbba: 'https://blogs.law.columbia.edu/climatechange/2025/07/07/the-one-big-beautiful-bill-act-considerations-for-cities-and-community-partners/',
  irs: 'https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-25-42.pdf',
  rutgers: 'https://njclimateresourcecenter.rutgers.edu/new-jerseys-temperatures-rise-by-4-degrees-fahrenheit-twice-the-global-average-since-1900/',
  ncics: 'https://statesummaries.ncics.org/chapter/nj/',
  imm: 'https://www.monitoringanalytics.com/reports/PJM_State_of_the_Market/2025/2025-som-pjm-vol1.pdf',
};

const questions = [
  'Will you have the borough assess every borough-owned roof and parking lot for solar within 90 days of taking office?',
  'Will you pursue at least one project that can be in service by December 31, 2027, so the borough can collect the federal clean electricity investment credit?',
  'Will you favor financing that costs taxpayers nothing up front, such as a power purchase agreement or New Jersey’s Energy Savings Improvement Program?',
  'Will you publish the borough’s annual electricity use and spending?',
  'In a sentence or two: what would you do to protect Roselle Park from rising energy costs and from storms like Ida?',
];

const evidence = [
  {
    name: 'PSE&G electricity rate history',
    href: '/solar/pseg-rates/',
    summary: 'What PSE&G has charged residential and commercial customers since 2016, from its own filed tariffs.',
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Accelerating solar for Roselle Park"
  description="Solar on borough property would cut Roselle Park's electric bill, can cost taxpayers nothing up front, and is the right thing to do. Federal money for it ends in 2027."
>
  <h1>Accelerating solar for Roselle Park</h1>
  <p class="meta">For the November 2026 election · updated September 2026</p>

  <p>
    I live in Roselle Park, and I’m asking both candidates for mayor, Joe Signorello Jr. and Jorge Ramirez, and everyone
    running for borough council, to commit to solar on borough property. The case rests on three things.
  </p>

  <h2>It cuts the borough’s electric bill</h2>
  <p>
    PSE&amp;G’s standard commercial energy rate went from 9.83¢ to 19.37¢ per kilowatt-hour between May 2023 and April
    2026, according to its own <a href="/solar/pseg-rates/">filed tariffs</a>. It nearly doubled in three years. The
    household rate rose 37% over the same span.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pressure hasn’t let up. PJM, the grid operator that sets wholesale capacity prices for New Jersey, has run three
    capacity auctions since mid-2025, and all three cleared between $325 and $334 per megawatt-day, more than 11 times the <a href={src.pjm2526} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">$28.92 price</a> of the 2024/25 auction. The
    last two fell more than 6,600 megawatts short of PJM’s reliability target
    (<a href={src.pjm2728} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2027/28</a>, <a href={src.pjm2829} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2028/29</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    PSE&amp;G household bills rose <a href={src.bgs2025} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">17% in June 2025</a> and held <a href={src.bgs2026} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">roughly flat in 2026</a>, in part because of
    a temporary cap on that auction. Power from panels on borough roofs has a price fixed for 15 years or more, by contract
    or by ownership, whatever happens to PJM.
  </p>

  <h2>It can cost taxpayers nothing up front, and federal money for it runs out in 2027</h2>
  <p>
    Roselle Park has done this before. In 2012 the Union County Improvement Authority put solar on 31 public buildings
    across 15 agencies, including the Roselle Park Board of Education. A developer owned the panels and sold the power
    below utility rates, and the county <a href={src.county} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">projected about $5 million in savings</a> over 15 years. The borough government wasn’t one of the 15.
  </p>
  <p>
    New Jersey also lets towns pay for solar out of the savings it produces, through the <a href={src.esip} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Energy Savings Improvement Program</a>, which
    requires every year of the deal to be cash-positive. The state’s solar incentive pays <a href={src.adi} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">$110 per megawatt-hour for 15 years</a> to
    rooftop and parking-canopy systems under 1 megawatt.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deadline is federal. Under the 2025 budget law, a solar project that didn’t start construction by July 4, 2026
    gets the clean electricity investment credit only if it’s <a href={src.obbba} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">running by December 31, 2027</a>. Local
    governments can take that credit as a direct cash payment, typically 30% of the project’s cost. Whoever takes office
    in January has twelve months.
  </p>

  <h2>It’s the right thing to do</h2>
  <p>
    New Jersey has warmed about 4°F since 1900, twice the global average, according to the <a href={src.rutgers} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Office of the New Jersey State Climatologist</a>.
    Ida killed 30 people in the state in 2021. Downpours of two inches or more were about <a href={src.ncics} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">50% more frequent</a> in 2005 to 2014 than the
    long-term average.
  </p>
  <p>
    On PJM’s grid, which supplies Roselle Park, coal-fired generation rose 19% in 2025, according to the grid’s <a href={src.imm} class="ext-inline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">independent market monitor</a>. Every
    kilowatt-hour made on a borough roof is one the town doesn’t buy from that grid.
  </p>

  <h2>What I’m asking candidates</h2>
  <ol class="asks">
    {questions.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
  </ol>

  <h2>Evidence</h2>
  <table class="projects">
    <tbody>
      {evidence.map((e) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><a href={e.href}>{e.name}</a></th>
          <td>{e.summary}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Base>
